# Line endings are normalised to LF in the index, so a file's committed bytes do not
# depend on which OS committed it.
#
# This matters beyond tidiness: CI regenerates the ARM conformance goldens and fails on
# any git diff. `regen` writes LF. If a case file were ever committed with CRLF from a
# Windows checkout, the Linux runner would regenerate it to LF and the diff check would
# fail on line endings alone - a red build reporting nothing about the corpus.
* text=auto

# The committed binary fixtures: guest executables, their reference ELFs, and the
# permissively-licensed conformance font. Marked explicitly rather than left to the
# text/binary heuristic, because a false "text" call would corrupt them on checkout.
*.velf binary
*.elf  binary
*.bin  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.png  binary

# >>> A SHELL SCRIPT MUST BE LF IN THE WORKING TREE, NOT ONLY IN THE INDEX.
#
# `text=auto` above normalises the INDEX, and on Windows the checkout then gets CRLF - at
# which point bash refuses the script's own first line: `set -euo pipefail` fails with
# "invalid option name", because the option name it sees ends in a carriage return. Every
# guest-suite `build.sh` carries a note telling the reader to strip the endings into /tmp
# first, and that workaround is itself broken: the scripts resolve their source directory
# from `$BASH_SOURCE`, so a copy in /tmp compiles nothing and reports a missing source file.
#
# The endings are the root cause, so they are fixed here rather than worked around in
# twenty scripts. Rebuilding a guest artifact is how a conformance app gets EXTENDED, and a
# build step that needs an undocumented incantation is a step nobody takes.
*.sh text eol=lf
